{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2107","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2107","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2107\",\"parent_revision\":\"1fcb9afb3d61331f00504c60010d4705961229e3\",\"root_revision\":\"d1a305d4266b1d8b3a00af7096d836e371b8d82a1ee4c681835c57d86271260b\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add post-pr ci-failure auto-fix watcher skill** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/developer-tools/no-mistakes.md \u2014 pattern: Automatic PR Opening and CI Watching (Key Features / Configuration auto_fix_ci flag)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-03\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-04)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\\\n\\\\n| Input | Status | Source |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Item description + target state |\\\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | New skill path + integration point explicitly named |\\\\n| External interfaces | PARTIAL | `gh` CLI confirmed; PR-number retrieval when no PR exists is MISSING |\\\\n| Constraints | DERIVABLE | Max-2-cycle cap stated; loop-prevention state storage design MISSING |\\\\n| Existing system context | AVAILABLE | `complete-implementation` lines 887\u2013905; kage-bunshin spawn.py; `dh:task-worker` dispatch pattern |\\\\n| Verification expectations | AVAILABLE | Measurable signal section fully specifies observable outcomes |\\\\n\\\\n**Missing inputs (affect detail design only \u2014 do not block planning):**\\\\n\\\\n1. **Autonomy gate**: Should the watcher spawn only when `autonomy=full_auto`, or always after every push?\\\\n2. **Loop-prevention state**: Where is the per-run fix-count stored? Options: ephemeral in session context; `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/state/ci-watcher-{run-id}.json`; GitHub PR comment as state store.\\\\n3. **PR number when no PR exists**: The watcher needs a fallback when the branch has no open PR.\\\\n4. **Polling cadence and timeout**: No cadence or give-up timeout stated in the item.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 CI failures that go unnoticed after push are a real usability gap in `full_auto` workflows. The skill is a quality-of-life multiplier for unattended automation runs. Not P0 because developers can still manually observe and fix; the pain is latency, not data loss.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Nothing currently depends on this skill \u2014 it is net-new capability.\\\\n- Bottleneck: Unattended `full_auto` runs that push code and then silently leave CI broken. The developer either misses the failure or must re-trigger manually.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- CI failures discovered and fixed automatically within the same `complete-implementation` session \u2014 zero developer re-invocation required.\\\\n- Closes the feedback loop on pushed branches: `complete-implementation` becomes end-to-end quality-gated, not just pre-push quality-gated.\\\\n- Provides a model for the broader `no-mistakes`-style `auto_fix_ci` pattern inside Claude Code skills (research entry: `research/developer-tools/no-mistakes.md` \u2014 `pr.auto_fix_ci` flag).\\\\n- Reduces PR review churn caused by CI failures the developer discovers late.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAfter `/complete-implementation` pushes a branch:\\\\n1. A background `claude -p` process starts running `/dh:ci-watcher #N` (N = backlog issue number).\\\\n2. The watcher polls `gh run list -R {repo} --branch {branch} --limit 1` until the run reaches a terminal state (success or failure).\\\\n3. On success: the watcher exits silently.\\\\n4. On failure: the watcher fetches `gh run view --log-failed`, dispatches `dh:task-worker` with the failure log, and waits for the fix commit to be pushed.\\\\n5. After the fix push, the watcher re-polls. If CI passes, it posts a PR comment summarising the auto-fix diff and exits.\\\\n6. After 2 fix cycles with continued failure, the watcher posts a PR comment stating auto-fix was not successful, then exits \u2014 no infinite loop.\\\\n7. The watcher is not user-invocable. It is spawned programmatically only from `complete-implementation`.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n- New skill file: `plugins/development-harness/skills/ci-watcher/SKILL.md` with `user-invocable: false`, `argument-hint: '#N'`, body documenting polling cadence, max cycle count, and escape hatches.\\\\n- Modification to `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md`: after the push step (lines 887\u2013905) and before Team Shutdown (line 909), add a conditional step that spawns the CI watcher as a background `claude -p` session using kage-bunshin `spawn.py`.\\\\n- The spawned session inherits `GITHUB_TOKEN` from the parent environment and has access to all MCP servers.\\\\n- A per-run state mechanism (design decision pending) tracks fix-cycle count to enforce the max-2 cap.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. After `/complete-implementation #N` push with `autonomy=full_auto`: `ps aux | grep ci-watcher` shows a live background process while the watcher is polling.\\\\n2. On a deliberately broken test in a pushed branch: the PR receives an automated comment (observable via `gh pr view --comments`) containing the fix diff summary after CI completes with failure.\\\\n3. The branch advances by exactly one auto-fix commit per failed CI run, maximum 2 auto-fix commits total (verify via `git log --oneline` on the branch after 2 CI failures).\\\\n4. After 2 failed CI fix cycles: the watcher posts a PR comment stating it could not auto-fix, then exits (verifiable via `ps aux` showing no watcher process).\\\\n5. Skill file `plugins/development-harness/skills/ci-watcher/SKILL.md` exists with frontmatter field `user-invocable: false`.\\\\n6. The `complete-implementation` final step section (lines 887\u2013905 region) documents the conditional spawn logic.\\n\\n### Human Input\\n\\nDesign decisions needed before detailed task decomposition:\\\\n1. **Autonomy gate**: Should CI watcher spawn only when `plan.autonomy == full_auto`, or on every push regardless of autonomy setting?\\\\n2. **Loop-prevention state store**: Where should per-run fix-cycle count be stored? Options: (a) ephemeral in session context only; (b) file at `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/state/ci-watcher-{run-id}.json`; (c) GitHub PR comment as the state store (idempotent, visible).\\\\n3. **No-PR fallback**: When the branch has no associated PR, should the watcher skip silently, open the PR automatically, or post a commit comment instead?\\\\n4. **Polling cadence and timeout**: Suggested 30-second poll interval and 20-minute hard timeout. Acceptable?\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Should the CI watcher spawn only when `autonomy=full_auto`, or after every push from `complete-implementation`?\\\\n- Where should the per-run fix-cycle count be stored to survive session restarts (ephemeral, state file, or PR comment)?\\\\n- What is the expected behavior when the branch has no associated PR (PR comment target unavailable)?\\\\n- Is a 30-second polling interval and 20-minute hard timeout acceptable for the watcher?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\\\n|------|------|\\\\n| Skill | /dh:complete-implementation (integration point: lines 887\u2013905) |\\\\n| Skill | /dh:kage-bunshin (spawn mechanism for background claude -p sessions) |\\\\n| Skill | /dh:dispatch (dispatch pattern reference \u2014 dh:task-worker required) |\\\\n| Agent | @dh:task-worker (fix dispatcher \u2014 must be used, not general-purpose) |\\\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/spawn.py` |\\\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` lines 887\u2013947 |\\\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-milestone/SKILL.md` (kage-bunshin spawn pattern in Steps 5c\u20136) |\\\\n| Research | `research/developer-tools/no-mistakes.md` \u2014 `pr.auto_fix_ci` configuration pattern |\\\\n| Rule | `.claude/rules/ci-workflows.md` (CI modification protocol) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: #1061 (effort-level flag for kage-bunshin spawn) \u2014 OPTIONAL. Watcher can spawn without `--effort` but the flag improves control. Not a hard blocker.\\\\n- Depends on: `gh` CLI available in the runtime environment (confirmed \u2014 CLAUDE.md documents `gh` usage with `-R` flag).\\\\n- Blocks: None currently.\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Design decisions in 'Questions for Human' must be answered before detailed task decomposition (loop-prevention store, autonomy gate, no-PR fallback).\\\\n- No hard tool or infrastructure blockers \u2014 all spawn mechanisms and `gh` CLI patterns are confirmed available.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 Two primary deliverables: (1) new `ci-watcher` SKILL.md (skill documentation, no Python scripts required for the base case); (2) modification to `complete-implementation` SKILL.md to add the spawn step after push. The fix-dispatch cycle reuses existing `dh:task-worker` + kage-bunshin patterns with no new infrastructure. Main complexity is polling loop logic and loop-prevention state design expressed as skill instructions. If a per-run state file is chosen for the loop-prevention store, a small Python script may be needed \u2014 this would shift to High.\\n\\n## Source\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nResearch entry: ./research/developer-tools/no-mistakes.md \u2014 auto_fix_ci PR config option.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAfter Final Step push, when an issue/PR is associated with the branch, `/complete-implementation` either (a) spawns a background `claude -p` session running a new `/dh:ci-watcher #N` skill or (b) registers a deferred task. The watcher polls `gh run list -R {repo} --branch {branch} --limit 1` until status is final, and on failure fetches `gh run view --log-failed`, dispatches `dh:task-worker` with the failure log, lets it apply a fix-and-push cycle (max 2 cycles to avoid loops), and posts a comment on the PR summarising what changed. New skill `plugins/development-harness/skills/ci-watcher/SKILL.md` documents the polling cadence, max cycle count, and escape hatches.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAfter `/complete-implementation #N` push with autonomy=full_auto: a background process `claude -p --skill /dh:ci-watcher` is spawned (verifiable via `ps aux | grep ci-watcher` while the watcher is alive). On a deliberately broken test, after CI completes with failure, the PR receives an automated comment with the fix diff, and the branch advances by exactly one auto-fix commit per failed run (max 2). Skill file `plugins/development-harness/skills/ci-watcher/SKILL.md` exists with frontmatter declaring `user-invocable: false`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe Final Step at lines 887\u2013905 of `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` commits and pushes, then proceeds to Team Shutdown (lines 909\u2013920) and the Final Handoff Output (lines 925\u2013947). After push, no skill in the repository monitors the GitHub Actions run for that branch. A grep across `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` and `plugins/*/skills/*/SKILL.md` for `gh run watch`, `monitor.*CI`, or `watch.*CI` returned zero matches. When CI fails after the developer has moved on, the failure stays unfixed until the developer notices and re-invokes a fix manually.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"995d6392ef4a66199b8ff5ad74e33200315e525a28c09c2ee979a0178425c6de\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtYOOA\"}"}